-/*
- * USB dual-role (OTG) bring-up.
- *
- * The gadget half gives us the ttyGS0 serial console; the host half lets a USB
- * keyboard/mouse be plugged in through an OTG adapter, which matters because
- * the touchscreen is not working yet.
- *
- * Wiring follows the in-tree mt8390-genio-common.dtsi pattern: mtu3 registers a
- * usb_role_switch for its own fwnode (the boolean "usb-role-switch" property is
- * what turns that on), and tcpm finds it by walking the fwnode graph from the
- * Type-C connector's endpoint (typec_con_hs <-> mtu3_hs_role_sw, already paired
- * in mt6895.dtsi) and drives it on every attach/detach.
- *
- * Three things mtu3 demands in OTG/HOST mode that are easy to get wrong
- * (mtu3_plat.c: ssusb_rscs_init):
- *   - maximum-speed: the USB3 half has never been brought up here, and
- *     keyboards/mice are USB2, so stay on high-speed.
- *   - vbus-supply = the MT6375 OTG 5V boost, i.e. otg_vbus.  otg_sx->vbus is
- *     fetched with a non-optional devm_regulator_get(), and in host mode this
- *     is what actually powers the peripheral.
- *   - **do NOT add `wakeup-source`.**  ssusb_wakeup_of_property_parse() returns
- *     early only when that property is absent; with it present mtu3 insists on
- *     a `mediatek,syscon-wakeup` phandle (SPM wakeup register) and the probe
- *     dies with -ENOENT ("failed to parse uwk property"), which takes the whole
- *     Type-C stack down with it: usb0 never binds, so tcpm's fwnode role-switch
- *     lookup fails to resolve and the TCPC defers forever
- *     ("mt6375-tcpc: Failed to register tcpci port"), and both the UDC and the
- *     Type-C port disappear.  We don't need USB remote wakeup.
